{
  "title": "Strings and slicing",
  "summary": "Index, slice, and transform text.",
  "minutes": 10,
  "steps": [
    {
      "type": "learn",
      "title": "Indexing, from both ends",
      "body": [
        "A `str` is an immutable sequence of characters. Index it like a C# string, but negative indexes count from the end:",
        "",
        "```python",
        "word = \"python\"",
        "print(word[0])    # p",
        "print(word[-1])   # n  (last)",
        "print(word[-2])   # o",
        "print(len(word))  # 6",
        "```",
        "",
        "There is no separate `char` type: `word[0]` is a `str` of length 1."
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "predict",
      "prompt": "What does this print?",
      "code": [
        "word = \"python\"",
        "print(word[1], word[-1], len(word))"
      ],
      "answer": "y n 6",
      "explain": "Index 1 is the second character. `-1` is always the last."
    },
    {
      "type": "learn",
      "title": "Slicing",
      "body": [
        "`s[start:stop:step]` takes a piece. `stop` is **excluded**, and every part is optional:",
        "",
        "```python",
        "s = \"abcdefgh\"",
        "s[2:5]    # 'cde'",
        "s[:3]     # 'abc'   first three",
        "s[-3:]    # 'fgh'   last three",
        "s[::2]    # 'aceg'  every second",
        "s[::-1]   # 'hgfedcba' reversed",
        "```",
        "",
        "Slices never raise `IndexError`: `s[5:100]` just gives what exists. It is `Substring` without the exceptions."
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "match",
      "prompt": "Match each slice of `s = \"abcdefgh\"` to its result.",
      "pairs": [
        [
          "`s[1:4]`",
          "`bcd`"
        ],
        [
          "`s[:2]`",
          "`ab`"
        ],
        [
          "`s[-2:]`",
          "`gh`"
        ],
        [
          "`s[::3]`",
          "`adg`"
        ],
        [
          "`s[::-1]`",
          "`hgfedcba`"
        ]
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "learn",
      "title": "String methods",
      "body": [
        "Strings are immutable, so methods return **new** strings:",
        "",
        "```python",
        "s = \"  Hello, World  \"",
        "s.strip()            # 'Hello, World'    (C# Trim)",
        "s.lower(), s.upper()",
        "s.replace(\"l\", \"L\")",
        "\"a,b,c\".split(\",\")   # ['a', 'b', 'c']",
        "\"-\".join([\"a\", \"b\"]) # 'a-b'  (string.Join, but called on the separator)",
        "\"ell\" in \"Hello\"     # True   (Contains)",
        "\"Hello\".startswith(\"He\")",
        "\"ha\" * 3             # 'hahaha'",
        "```"
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "predict",
      "prompt": "What does this print?",
      "code": [
        "s = \"  Data, Science  \"",
        "parts = s.strip().lower().split(\", \")",
        "print(parts)",
        "print(\"+\".join(parts))",
        "print(\"sci\" in s)"
      ],
      "answer": [
        "['data', 'science']",
        "data+science",
        "False"
      ],
      "explain": "`strip` then `lower` then `split` chain left to right. `in` is case-sensitive, and the original `s` still has a capital S."
    },
    {
      "type": "blanks",
      "prompt": "Make `initials` hold `G.H.` for `\"grace hopper\"`.",
      "code": [
        "name = \"grace hopper\"",
        "first, last = name.__1__()",
        "initials = f\"{first[__2__].upper()}.{last[0].__3__()}.\""
      ],
      "blanks": [
        "split",
        "0",
        "upper"
      ],
      "bank": [
        "strip",
        "-1",
        "lower",
        "1"
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "mcq",
      "prompt": "What does `\"hello\"[1] = \"a\"` do?",
      "options": [
        "Raises `TypeError`: strings are immutable",
        "Changes the string to `hallo`",
        "Returns a new string `hallo`",
        "Raises `IndexError`"
      ],
      "answer": 0,
      "explain": "Like C#, strings cannot be changed in place. Build a new one: `s[:1] + \"a\" + s[2:]`."
    },
    {
      "type": "learn",
      "title": "Formatting with f-strings",
      "body": [
        "After a `:` inside the braces comes a **format spec**, close to C#'s `{x,10:F2}`:",
        "",
        "```python",
        "f\"{3.14159:.2f}\"   # '3.14'",
        "f\"{42:5}\"          # '   42'   width 5",
        "f\"{'ab':<5}|\"      # 'ab   |'  left-align",
        "f\"{'ab':>5}|\"      # '   ab|'  right-align",
        "f\"{'ab':^6}|\"      # '  ab  |' centre",
        "f\"{1234567:,}\"     # '1,234,567'",
        "f\"{0.256:.1%}\"     # '25.6%'",
        "```"
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "code",
      "title": "Username maker",
      "prompt": [
        "Read a full name (one line, first and last name separated by a space, possibly with extra spaces around it). Print a username: the first letter of the first name plus the whole last name, all lowercase. Then print it reversed, and its length right-aligned in a field of 4 between bars.",
        "",
        "For `  Ada Lovelace `:",
        "",
        "```text",
        "alovelace",
        "ecalevola",
        "|   9|",
        "```"
      ],
      "starter": "name = input()\n",
      "solution": [
        "name = input()",
        "first, last = name.strip().split()",
        "user = (first[0] + last).lower()",
        "print(user)",
        "print(user[::-1])",
        "print(f\"|{len(user):>4}|\")"
      ],
      "tests": [
        {
          "name": "Ada Lovelace",
          "stdin": "  Ada Lovelace \n",
          "expect": [
            "alovelace",
            "ecalevola",
            "|   9|"
          ]
        },
        {
          "name": "Alan Turing",
          "stdin": "Alan Turing\n",
          "expect": [
            "aturing",
            "gniruta",
            "|   7|"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "hints": [
        "`strip()` then `split()` gives the two names.",
        "`[::-1]` reverses, `:>4` right-aligns in 4 characters."
      ]
    }
  ]
}
